messages:
support-jen: Hey team — customer reported duplicate billing for a short burst (trace: tx_8f3c2a). They say their app retried on a 502 and ended up charged twice for the same user action. Node env: v18.16, undici: 5.12.0, @redwood/sdk-ts: 1.4.2. Logs attached below.

eng-matt: Can you paste the minimal repro or request logs? Do we have the request id/correlation id from the backend?

support-jen: Pasted the relevant log lines — looks like two requests from the same client within 600ms:
```
[2026-03-10T15:04:01.234Z] req=start id=req_abc123 trace=tx_8f3c2a
[2026-03-10T15:04:01.832Z] upstream=502 req=req_abc123
[2026-03-10T15:04:01.837Z] client-retry attempt=2 req=req_abc123
[2026-03-10T15:04:02.045Z] req=processed id=req_def456 trace=tx_8f3c2a
```
support-jen: Customer also shared their client snippet (minimal):
```js
// BAD: reusing the same AbortController across attempts
const ac = new AbortController();
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: JSON.stringify({prompt}),
      signal: ac.signal,
      headers: {Authorization: `Bearer ${KEY}`}
    });
    if (res.ok) return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    // backoff then retry
  }
}
```
support-jen: They said they used the SDK's retry wrapper plus a per-request timeout that calls `ac.abort()` on timeout.

devx-alyssa: Reusing one AbortController is a common footgun. If the controller gets aborted after the first attempt, subsequent retries see an already-aborted signal and behave oddly. Also, with undici/fetch the body stream can't be replayed — the SDK may re-send a new request but the server could have already processed the first attempt.

eng-matt: Quick repro note: if first request reaches our backend and we start processing, then client aborts, undici may still have sent the packets; retry will POST again. Without an idempotency key the server treats it as a new request -> double charge.

oncall-ryan: Action items: 1) tell customers to create a fresh AbortController per attempt or use SDK timeout helpers; 2) add server-side idempotency guard for critical endpoints; 3) treat POSTs without idempotency tokens as non-retriable by default in the SDK.

eng-matt: I pushed a small SDK change in branch fix/retry-abort-guard — summary: we now create an internal AbortController per retry attempt when user passes a `timeout` option, and we avoid retrying POSTs unless caller explicitly sets `idempotencyKey` or `allowRetry: true`. Patch: https://git.internal/redwood/sdk/commit/9d3b7f2

devx-alyssa: Nice. Also logging: added more context to retry logs so support can map backend trace -> client attempt (correlation header `x-rdwd-attempt`). Will ship in 1.4.3.

support-jen: Sent guidance to the customer (recommended upgrade to 1.4.3 when published, in the meantime: use new AbortController per attempt + attach an idempotency key). Customer responded with a repro showing reduced duplicate events after changing their code. :relieved:

customer-bot: Repro result: after change, only one server-side processing event for trace tx_8f3c2a2. No duplicate billing observed.

eng-matt: Note for ops — we should still consider server-side de-dup for a period while clients upgrade. Filed INFRA-3412 to add per-account idempotency window for write endpoints.

oncall-ryan: Thanks all. I'll follow up if more reports come in. Closing for now. :thumbsup:
